  hand-built likewise; no other sample of this variant was found
  3b1af90209ea81074f5092520430cc67c7db2a26908fd9fee2da239ae47dcfcb
implode-hand-8k-3trees.zip  | 6 implode, 8K window, 3 trees (flags 6)
  hand-built likewise
  4e4aec7f5057a5b8f217e75fb43537c02f355121da3d4bb43bee9f218774aca9

The four hand-built zips hold one entry, hand.txt, the 9400 bytes of
`hand_data()` (SHA-256 5079ff3e18949acca7b9a31962b516f5e2ffc93fe58282e45a3a8278e19f52e1),
imploded by `implode()` in src/infozip/tests/explode.rs over fixed complete
Shannon-Fano trees (literals 7/8/9 bits, lengths 4/6/8, distances 6), greedy
matches including distances past 4K and lengths with the extra byte.
Authored for this repository (MIT). Made with
  STUFFR_INFOZIP_HAND_OUT=$W/hand cargo test -p stuffr-formats --lib \
    infozip::tests::explode::write_hand_fixtures -- --ignored
and accepted only after both references passed them:
  /usr/bin/unzip -t $W/hand/<zip>                       (exit 0, "OK")
  docker run --rm -v $W/hand:/w ubuntu:24.04 sh -c 'apt-get update -qq &&
    apt-get install -qq -y unzip >/dev/null && unzip -t /w/<zip>'
                                                       (exit 0, "OK")
Both references' `unzip -p` give the SHA-256 above for all four.
`explode_all_four_variants` checks the writer still makes them byte for byte.

To reproduce all thirteen hashes: run `shasum -a 256 *.zip` in this directory.

Unsupported by design: exact answers of `unzip -t <file>`
----------------------------------------------------------
All four print the same shape; only the "can do" version differs. unzip
refuses on the version-needed field (PK compat.) BEFORE it looks at the method.

  aes128.zip, aes256.zip   (needs v5.1)
    Apple   exit 81
      Archive:  aes128.zip
         skipping: README                  need PK compat. v5.1 (can do v4.5)
      Caution:  zero files tested in aes128.zip.
      1 file skipped because of unsupported compression or encoding.
    Ubuntu  exit 81, identical except "(can do v4.6)"
  lzma.zip                 (needs v6.3, entry vimrc)
    Apple   exit 81, "skipping: vimrc  need PK compat. v6.3 (can do v4.5)", same footer
    Ubuntu  exit 81, same with "(can do v4.6)"
  bzip2.zip                (needs v4.6, entry sample.txt)
    Apple   exit 81, "skipping: sample.txt  need PK compat. v4.6 (can do v4.5)", same footer
    Ubuntu  exit 0: "testing: sample.txt   OK" and
            "No errors detected in compressed data of bzip2.zip."

Coverage gaps
-------------
 * Implode 4K+3 trees and 8K+2 trees: no sample exists in any corpus found;
   hand-built in Task 5 (plan ruling P5): implode-hand-*.zip above.
 * deflate64 match distance > 32 KiB in deflate64-lorem.zip: verified in
   Task 4 (P6). The longest distance the stream uses is 65521 (counted by
   the port, `deflate64_fixture`), so it reaches distance code 31
   (49153-65536); code 30 (32769-49152) is not shown to be reached, and
   no hand-built stream was made for it.
 * zip2's deflate64_issue_25.zip was rejected: both references reject it
   (exit 3, "missing 2166816915 bytes in zipfile").

inflate damage table (stuffr 0.16.0, Task 4)
============================================

inflate_damage.txt records what Info-ZIP's own inflate.c (UnZip 6.0; Apple's
zip-33 copy is byte-identical to upstream's) does with 200 damaged
deflate/deflate64 streams: return code, MESSAGE() calls, every flushed chunk
length and the SHA-256 of the flushed bytes. `codes_on_damage_match_the_c`
(src/infozip/tests.rs) regenerates each input and requires the port to match
every row. Authored for this repository (MIT); the generated bytes carry no
other licence.

Bases (damage is applied to these; frozen so a flate2 change moves nothing):

  inflate_damage/text.deflate    flate2 1.1.10 (miniz_oxide 0.9.1) level 6,
                                 text(100000, 21)
    348056711d816afada70b1f46bf56904315d60e86d618669109beacb031c05e2
  inflate_damage/mixed.deflate   level 1, mixed(40000, 22)
    47c90865c94abed6429107ac7ffe91d2129f635098d81f9c0e246e00df6173ca
  inflate_damage/stored.deflate  level 0, random(3000, 23)
    44e9b9f141ab05d94ea8d9bcaad78cc70843e0ec4daa421d4c1fae82fd339862
  inflate_damage/fixed.deflate   level 9, text(40, 24): one fixed block
    2ed48559812db493c164d56d4a3f24e88cb86abd14f739f2c95215fb1df9033b
  lorem64                        the entry data of deflate64-lorem.zip
  inflate_damage.txt
    8fc91eb5d08c01cedb0ee1254ac330ba18c7d92e0fbb725141ee1c04165a75da

Damage: input i (0..199) takes base i % 5 and a XorShift seeded
(i + 1) * 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15 picks bit flips (1-3), a truncation, or
inserted bytes (1-2); see `damage()`. Each row also records the input's
SHA-256, so a generator change shows as a mismatch, not a silent pass.

Recipe (macOS, Apple clang 21.0.0; $W any scratch directory, $UZ a copy of
Apple's zip-zip-33/unzip/unzip60):

  1. Bases (once; committed):
       cargo test -p stuffr-formats --features deflate --lib \
         infozip::tests::inflate::write_damage_bases -- --ignored
  2. Inputs and the driver's manifest:
       STUFFR_INFOZIP_DAMAGE_OUT=$W/damage cargo test -p stuffr-formats --lib \
         infozip::tests::inflate::write_damage_inputs -- --ignored
  3. The driver: driver.c below, beside a stub quarantine.h holding only
     `typedef void *qtn_file_t;` (unix/unxcfg.h includes Apple's private
     header), compiled with the real inflate.c and Apple's macosx target's
     defines:
       cc -O0 -w -DUNIX -DBSD -DLARGE_FILE_SUPPORT -DUNICODE_SUPPORT \
         -I$W/stubinc -I$UZ -o $W/driver $W/driver.c $UZ/inflate.c
     Built again with -DNOMEMCPY (Ubuntu's DEFINES; the only one inflate.c
     reads) it prints a byte-identical table.
  4. The table:
       { echo '# inflate.c (UnZip 6.0, Apple zip-33 = upstream) on 200 damaged deflate/deflate64 streams.'
         echo '# Generated by the C driver in README ("inflate_damage.txt"); do not edit by hand.'
         echo '# NNN|base|kind|input-sha256|rc|messages|total|chunks|output-sha256'
         $W/driver $W/damage/manifest.txt; } > inflate_damage.txt

  The hand-built edge cases in `edge_cases_match_the_c` came from the same
  driver over `write_hand_inputs`' manifest-hand.txt (same command, same
  output columns); their answers are written into the test.

driver.c:

  /* inflate_damage driver: Info-ZIP UnZip 6.0's real inflate.c (Apple zip-33,
   * identical to upstream) over a stub G.  Reads manifest lines
   *   NNN|base|d64|kind|in_sha256|path
   * and prints one table row per input:
   *   NNN|base|kind|in_sha256|rc|msgs|total|chunks|out_sha256          */
  #define UNZIP_INTERNAL
  #include "unzip.h"
  #include <CommonCrypto/CommonDigest.h>

  Uz_Globs G;                       /* globals.h: extern Uz_Globs G (!REENTRANT) */
  ZCONST unsigned near mask_bits[17] = {
      0x0000, 0x0001, 0x0003, 0x0007, 0x000f, 0x001f, 0x003f, 0x007f, 0x00ff,
      0x01ff, 0x03ff, 0x07ff, 0x0fff, 0x1fff, 0x3fff, 0x7fff, 0xffff};

  static CC_SHA256_CTX out_sha;
  static unsigned long total;
  static char chunks[1 << 16];
  static char msgs[256];

  int flush(uch *rawbuf, ulg size, int unshrink) {
      char n[32];
      CC_SHA256_Update(&out_sha, rawbuf, (CC_LONG)size);
      total += size;
      snprintf(n, sizeof n, "%s%lu", chunks[0] ? "," : "", (unsigned long)size);
      strlcat(chunks, n, sizeof chunks);
      return 0;
  }
  int memflush(ZCONST uch *rawbuf, ulg size) { abort(); }
  int readbyte(void) { G.incnt = 0; return EOF; }   /* csize exhausted: sticky */
  static int message(zvoid *pG, uch *buf, ulg size, int flag) {
      if (flag != 1) abort();
      strlcat(msgs, !strcmp((char *)buf, "(incomplete l-tree)  ") ? "l-tree"
                  : !strcmp((char *)buf, "(incomplete d-tree)  ") ? "d-tree" : "?",
              sizeof msgs);
      return 0;
  }

  int main(int argc, char **argv) {
      char line[4096];
      FILE *m = fopen(argv[1], "r");
      while (fgets(line, sizeof line, m)) {
          char *nnn = strtok(line, "|"), *base = strtok(NULL, "|"),
               *d64 = strtok(NULL, "|"), *kind = strtok(NULL, "|"),
               *insha = strtok(NULL, "|"), *path = strtok(NULL, "|\n");
          static uch in[1 << 20];
          FILE *f = fopen(path, "rb");
          size_t n = fread(in, 1, sizeof in, f);
          unsigned char dig[CC_SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];
          int rc, i;
          fclose(f);
          memset(&G, 0, sizeof G);    /* a fresh G: zeroed slide, no fixed tables */
          G.message = message;
          G.inptr = in;
          G.incnt = (int)n;
          total = 0; chunks[0] = 0; msgs[0] = 0;
          CC_SHA256_Init(&out_sha);
          rc = inflate(atoi(d64));
          CC_SHA256_Final(dig, &out_sha);
          printf("%s|%s|%s|%s|%d|%s|%lu|%s|", nnn, base, kind, insha, rc,
                 msgs[0] ? msgs : "-", total, chunks[0] ? chunks : "-");
          for (i = 0; i < CC_SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++) printf("%02x", dig[i]);
          printf("\n");
      }
      return 0;
  }

explode and unshrink damage tables (stuffr 0.16.0, Task 5)
=========================================================

explode_damage.txt and unshrink_damage.txt record what Info-ZIP's own
explode.c (with inflate.c's huft_build) and unshrink.c (UnZip 6.0; Apple's
zip-33 copies are byte-identical to upstream's, and Ubuntu's patch series
touches neither) do with 200 damaged streams each: return code, (explode)
G.used_csize for a 5, every flushed chunk length and the SHA-256 of the
flushed bytes. `damage_codes_match_the_c` in src/infozip/tests/explode.rs
and .../unshrink.rs regenerates each input and requires the port to match
every row. Authored for this repository (MIT).

Bases: explode cycles through the entries of implode-4k-2trees.zip,
implode-8k-3trees.zip and the four implode-hand-*.zip; unshrink through
shrink-commons.zip's two entries and shrink-full-table.zip's one.

  explode_damage.txt
    f90e3de0d5b0b16292fcbc9e2e7becde14d361a5c65f95602768d1f630a9a547
  unshrink_damage.txt
    9ff050a962579a0016ab6d292615c7d408be9db30b8961db86b4d9d517bd20b2

Damage: input i takes base i % (bases) and a XorShift seeded
(i + 1) * 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15 picks bit flips (1-3), a truncation where the
entry's data ends, a truncation where the ARCHIVE ends (the cut bytes still
claimed: G.csize stays positive), inserted bytes (1-2), or (explode only) a
changed uncompressed size; see `damage()`. The window (G.area) starts as
zeros for even rows and 0xAA for odd ones. Each row records the input's
SHA-256, so a generator change shows as a mismatch, not a silent pass.

Recipe (macOS, Apple clang 21.0.0; $W any scratch directory, $UZ a copy of
Apple's zip-zip-33/unzip/unzip60):

  1. Inputs and manifests:
       STUFFR_INFOZIP_DAMAGE_OUT=$W/damage cargo test -p stuffr-formats --lib \
         infozip::tests -- --ignored write_damage_inputs write_hand_inputs
     (writes $W/damage/{explode,unshrink,explode-hand,unshrink-hand}/)
  2. The drivers: the three files below, beside the stub quarantine.h of
     the inflate recipe, compiled with the real sources and Apple's macosx
     target's defines:
       cc -O0 -w -DUNIX -DBSD -DLARGE_FILE_SUPPORT -DUNICODE_SUPPORT \
         -I$W/stubinc -I$UZ -o $W/explode_driver $W/explode_driver.c \
         $UZ/explode.c $UZ/inflate.c
       cc -O0 -w -DUNIX -DBSD -DLARGE_FILE_SUPPORT -DUNICODE_SUPPORT \
         -I$W/stubinc -I$UZ -o $W/unshrink_driver $W/unshrink_driver.c \
         $UZ/unshrink.c
     Built again with -DNOMEMCPY (Ubuntu's DEFINES; explode.c reads it), and
     at -O2, the explode driver prints a byte-identical table. In this build
     G.area is 65536 bytes with Parent at 0, value at 32768, Stack at 40960,
     shrint an int, OUTBUFSIZ 65536 (checked with a sizeof probe).
  3. The tables:
       { echo '# explode.c (UnZip 6.0, Apple zip-33 = upstream) on 200 damaged implode streams.'
         echo '# Generated by the C driver in README ("explode and unshrink damage tables"); do not edit by hand.'
         echo '# NNN|base|kind|input-sha256|rc|used_csize|total|chunks|output-sha256'
         $W/explode_driver $W/damage/explode/manifest.txt; } > explode_damage.txt
       { echo '# unshrink.c (UnZip 6.0, Apple zip-33 = upstream) on 200 damaged shrink streams.'
         echo '# Generated by the C driver in README ("explode and unshrink damage tables"); do not edit by hand.'
         echo '# NNN|base|kind|input-sha256|rc|total|chunks|output-sha256'
         $W/unshrink_driver $W/damage/unshrink/manifest.txt; } > unshrink_damage.txt

  The hand-built cases in each file's `hand_built_streams` came from the same
  drivers over the *-hand/manifest.txt; their answers are written into the
  tests.

common.h:

  /* Shared by the explode and unshrink damage drivers: Info-ZIP UnZip 6.0's
   * real explode.c / unshrink.c over a stub G.  Manifest lines:
   *   NNN|base|flags|ucsize|missing|fill|kind|in_sha256|path
   * flags = general-purpose bit flag, ucsize = G.lrec.ucsize, missing = bytes
   * the entry claims beyond the file (G.csize: a truncated archive, read()
   * gives 0), fill = the byte G.area starts as (hex).                       */
  #define UNZIP_INTERNAL
  #include "unzip.h"
  #include <CommonCrypto/CommonDigest.h>

  Uz_Globs G;
  ZCONST unsigned near mask_bits[17] = {
      0x0000, 0x0001, 0x0003, 0x0007, 0x000f, 0x001f, 0x003f, 0x007f, 0x00ff,
      0x01ff, 0x03ff, 0x07ff, 0x0fff, 0x1fff, 0x3fff, 0x7fff, 0xffff};

  static CC_SHA256_CTX out_sha;
  static unsigned long total;
  static char chunks[1 << 16];

  int flush(uch *rawbuf, ulg size, int unshrink) {
      char n[32];
      CC_SHA256_Update(&out_sha, rawbuf, (CC_LONG)size);
      total += size;
      snprintf(n, sizeof n, "%s%lu", chunks[0] ? "," : "", (unsigned long)size);
      strlcat(chunks, n, sizeof chunks);
      return 0;
  }
  int memflush(ZCONST uch *rawbuf, ulg size) { abort(); }
  /* fileio.c readbyte(), less the file: past the data, either the entry is
   * exhausted (G.csize <= 0: decrement, as unzip does "for tests done after
   * exploding") or the archive is (read() gives 0: G.csize untouched). */
  int readbyte(void) {
      if (G.csize <= 0) { G.csize--; G.incnt = 0; return EOF; }
      G.incnt = 0;
      return EOF;
  }
  static int message(zvoid *pG, uch *buf, ulg size, int flag) { abort(); }

  static min_info info;
  static uch in[1 << 20];

  /* One manifest line: sets up G and returns its fields. */
  static int setup(char *line, char **nnn, char **base, char **kind, char **insha) {
      char *flags, *ucsize, *missing, *fill, *path;
      FILE *f;
      size_t n;
      *nnn = strtok(line, "|"); *base = strtok(NULL, "|");
      flags = strtok(NULL, "|"); ucsize = strtok(NULL, "|");
      missing = strtok(NULL, "|"); fill = strtok(NULL, "|");
      *kind = strtok(NULL, "|"); *insha = strtok(NULL, "|");
      path = strtok(NULL, "|\n");
      f = fopen(path, "rb");
      n = fread(in, 1, sizeof in, f);
      fclose(f);
      memset(&G, 0, sizeof G);
      memset(&G.area, (int)strtol(fill, NULL, 16), sizeof G.area);
      G.message = message;
      memset(&info, 0, sizeof info);
      G.pInfo = &info;
      if (!G.outbuf) G.outbuf = malloc(OUTBUFSIZ + 1);
      G.inptr = in;
      G.incnt = (int)n;
      G.csize = atoll(missing);
      G.lrec.csize = n + atoll(missing);
      G.lrec.ucsize = strtoull(ucsize, NULL, 10);
      G.lrec.general_purpose_bit_flag = (ush)atoi(flags);
      /* extract_or_test_member() */
      G.bits_left = 0; G.bitbuf = 0L; G.zipeof = 0;
      total = 0; chunks[0] = 0;
      CC_SHA256_Init(&out_sha);
      return 0;
  }

  static void finish(void) {
      unsigned char dig[CC_SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH];
      int i;
      CC_SHA256_Final(dig, &out_sha);
      printf("%lu|%s|", total, chunks[0] ? chunks : "-");
      for (i = 0; i < CC_SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++) printf("%02x", dig[i]);
      printf("\n");
  }

explode_driver.c:

  /* explode_damage driver: prints NNN|base|kind|in_sha256|rc|used_csize|total|chunks|out_sha256
   * (used_csize only for rc 5, else "-"). */
  #include "common.h"
  int explode(__GPRO);
  int main(int argc, char **argv) {
      char line[4096], *nnn, *base, *kind, *insha;
      FILE *m = fopen(argv[1], "r");
      while (fgets(line, sizeof line, m)) {
          int rc;
          setup(line, &nnn, &base, &kind, &insha);
          rc = explode();
          printf("%s|%s|%s|%s|%d|", nnn, base, kind, insha, rc);
          if (rc == 5) printf("%lld|", (long long)G.used_csize); else printf("-|");
          finish();
      }
      return 0;
  }

unshrink_driver.c:

  /* unshrink_damage driver: prints NNN|base|kind|in_sha256|rc|total|chunks|out_sha256 */
  #include "common.h"
  int unshrink(__GPRO);
  int main(int argc, char **argv) {
      char line[4096], *nnn, *base, *kind, *insha;
      FILE *m = fopen(argv[1], "r");
      while (fgets(line, sizeof line, m)) {
          int rc;
          setup(line, &nnn, &base, &kind, &insha);
          rc = unshrink();
          printf("%s|%s|%s|%s|%d|", nnn, base, kind, insha, rc);
          finish();
      }
      return 0;
  }
